@param String sourceContext = "as_built_dimension_example"
<section id="as-built-dimensions" class="drawing-dimensions" aria-labelledby="dimension-example-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">Illustrated reading exercise</span>
    <h2 id="dimension-example-title">Which number measures which part?</h2>
    <figure>
        <a href="/images/guides/septic-as-built-dimensions-explained.png" data-track-click="nav" data-track-source-context="${sourceContext}" data-track-target-type="drawing_image" aria-label="Open the septic drawing dimensions example at full size">
            <img src="/images/guides/septic-as-built-dimensions-explained.webp" width="1440" height="1100" loading="lazy" alt="Fictional septic drawing: a 20-foot offset runs from house corner A to tank edge B; two separately labeled field runs are 60 and 80 feet. A 1,000-gallon tank label is capacity, not distance or bedroom approval." style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #d7ddd5;border-radius:8px;">
        </a>
        <figcaption>SepticPath educational illustration, not a property record or construction plan. Not to scale. The numbers are invented to demonstrate reading units and endpoints. <a href="/images/guides/septic-as-built-dimensions-explained.png" data-track-click="nav" data-track-source-context="${sourceContext}" data-track-target-type="drawing_image">Open the full-size diagram →</a></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Read both endpoints:</strong> the 20 ft label measures A to B, not the distance to the tank lid, center or nearest drainfield trench.</li>
        <li><strong>Add only the labeled runs:</strong> 60 + 80 = 140 linear feet in this example. That does not give area, trench width, soil loading or approved capacity.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep unlike units separate:</strong> 1,000 gallons describes the tank. It does not establish the permitted bedroom count or gallons-per-day design flow.</li>
        <li><strong>Check what the file proves:</strong> identify planned versus installed work and read the approval pages. Missing or ambiguous endpoints remain unresolved.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A historic sketch can guide a professional’s investigation; it does not locate today’s components precisely enough to authorize digging.</p>
</section>
